course: dictionary
lesson: 2889
title: A sleepy animal in context
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A dormouse is a small furry animal, a bit like a mouse but with a long, fluffy tail. You can work out what it is from the clues around it. «The tiny hazel dormouse curls into a tight golden ball to sleep through winter.» Tiny, curls and sleep through winter all tell you it is small and sleeps for a long time.
explain: Clues can also come from what the animal does. «The Dormouse's three sisters live at the bottom of a well, on treacle.» Treacle is not a word you need to know in advance: the sisters live on it, and a well is not a place for a nice snack, so you can guess it is something to eat. A dictionary then tells you it is thick sweet syrup.
explain: In «Alice in Wonderland», the Dormouse keeps falling asleep at the tea party. Sleepy as a dormouse is a phrase that means very sleepy. Once you know the story, the phrase makes sense.
fact: A dormouse is a small furry animal with a long, fluffy tail.
fact: Dormice live in woods and sleep through the winter. This is called hibernating.
fact: Hazel dormice are rare and protected in the UK and Europe.
fact: «Treacle» means thick sweet syrup, like golden syrup or molasses.
fact: Sleepy as a dormouse means very sleepy.
exercise: «The tiny hazel dormouse curls into a tight golden ball to sleep through winter.» What is a dormouse? | A fish that swims in rivers | *A small furry animal with a long, fluffy tail, which lives in woods | A sweet made of treacle | A large bird that flies south for the winter
exercise: «The Dormouse's three sisters live on treacle.» What is treacle? | Dry, cracked earth | A kind of bird | *Thick sweet syrup | Cold, salty water
exercise: Which is the plural of dormouse? | dormi | mouses | *dormice | dormouses
test: «The Dormouse keeps falling asleep at the tea party.» What does sleepy as a dormouse suggest? | Hungry for cake | *Very sleepy | Wide awake and cheerful | Lost in the forest
test: What does hibernating mean, as in «to sleep through winter (hibernates)»? | Running fast across the hills | Building a nest in a tree | Eating all day in the summer | *Sleeping through the winter
exam: «For the Dormouse, I breathe when I sleep and I sleep when I breathe really are the same, because he is nearly always asleep.» What does nearly always asleep tell you about the Dormouse? | *He sleeps most of the time | He never sleeps at all | He is only asleep at night | He is always wide awake
